#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101706} :activity/id #uuid "55a62405-baa9-5145-b156-afa7cad6d735" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049486} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101706 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102031 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2y.\n$$\n\nHow can we verify whether a given function is a **solution** of this equation? For example, is $y=e^{2x}$ a solution?\n\nTo check whether a given function $y$ satisfies a differential equation, we calculate the derivative and plug the result into the equation.\n\nFor $y=e^{2x},$ the derivative is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2e^{2x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now substitute the above and our expression for $y,$ into the original equation and see if we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2y \\\\ \\underbrace{2{e}^{2x}}_{{y}^{'}} & =2\\cdot \\underbrace{{e}^{2x}}_{y} \\\\ 2{e}^{2x} & =2{e}^{2x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe get a true statement! Therefore, $y=e^{2x}$ is a solution to the original equation.\n\n**Note:** There are often infinitely many solutions to a given differential equation! We will learn how to solve differential equations like the one shown above in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "b27482c3-602c-5761-9cd5-bec55b2c2932" :tutorial/math-academy-id 947 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9f3bfabf-d5b6-5551-9be2-cc7252c84684" :step/math-academy-id 1855 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101707}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101707 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100888 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100725 :question/id #uuid "e092e191-84ac-5d9f-b0db-94d628a6154e" :question/math-academy-id "e-525" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny' = -\\dfrac{y}{x}, \\qquad x \\neq 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f'(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$\n\n- Substituting $y = f(x)$ and $y' = f'(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\frac{1}{x})}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ and $y' = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049592} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050690} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050789}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186577229} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494099} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186577195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186577202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186577207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186577218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186577227}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"} :step/id #uuid "258fc829-0dbe-5a4d-a934-b53a4e674750" :step/math-academy-id 1856 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101812}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101795 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100951 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100795 :question/id #uuid "f5ffa549-e77e-5db4-9fde-5b0134957539" :question/math-academy-id "e-8732" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{3y}{x}, \\qquad y(-1) = -2.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y = Ax^3$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y = Ax^3$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(-1) = -2.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y({\\color{red}{-1}}) = {\\color{blue}{-2}}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{x}^{3} \\\\ -2 & =A(-1)^{3} \\\\ -2 & =A(-1) \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = 2x^3 .\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82a9b23c-4c79-56cd-9761-79d0ee22646d" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493367} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272211} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493365}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Initial Value Problems"} :step/id #uuid "3abf1ec6-68c5-532c-84ed-1d835930fc83" :step/math-academy-id 32470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101812 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100959 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100801 :question/id #uuid "1228900d-87d4-5ff0-8e01-ff9417e82220" :question/math-academy-id "e-9978" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = y.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y = e^x + 1$\n\n- $y = -e^x$\n\n- $y = 2e^x$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y = e^x + 1,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ {e}^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}{e}^{x}+1 \\\\ 0 & \\ne 1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false*. Therefore, the function $y = e^x+1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y = -e^x,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = -e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ -{e}^{x} & =-{e}^{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y = -e^x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y = 2e^x,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ 2{e}^{x} & =2{e}^{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y = 2e^x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494225} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494220}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"} :step/id #uuid "af2b5660-43ec-5c1d-8234-7fbe11b166c6" :step/math-academy-id 37572 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101795}}] :activity/title "Verifying Solutions of Differential Equations" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101045}]}
